<TopicPage slug="subjects/environmental-science/intermediate/energy-systems-and-the-grid">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    An electricity grid is <strong>the largest machine ever built</strong> — a continent-spanning
    circuit balanced in real time. The energy transition is its first full redesign since
    electrification: replacing fuel with weather, and shifting the operational challenge from
    the logistics of fuel to the engineering of flexibility. The resource lesson behind it:
    all energy is mining. Fossil fuels are ancient sunlight stored as buried carbon (coal from
    Carboniferous forests, oil and gas from marine plankton); uranium from ore bodies; even
    solar panels and wind turbines are built from mined materials. <strong>Energy
    resources</strong> are rock and ore in another form — which is why this lesson keeps
    returning to geology, extraction and their consequences.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the system, the trilemma, the cost revolution, the hard parts" widget="equationStep" config={energyCfg} />

  <h2>Energy resources: the menu and its arithmetic</h2>
  <p>
    The primary sources divide by what they burn, spin or capture. <strong>Fossil fuels</strong>
    (coal, oil, gas — about 80% of world primary energy) are energy-dense, storable,
    dispatchable — and the CO₂ is the problem: carbon that took hundreds of millions of years
    to bury is returned to the atmosphere in centuries. <strong>Nuclear</strong> (about 10% of
    electricity) splits uranium in a controlled chain reaction — dense beyond everything else
    (a fingertip of uranium holds the energy of about a tonne of coal), low-carbon, with a
    waste problem that is small in volume and long in reputation. <strong>Hydro</strong> (about
    15% of electricity) stores gravity: the cheapest electricity wherever the geography
    cooperates, and the largest energy-storage fleet on Earth (pumped storage).
    <strong>Wind and solar</strong> harvest flows rather than stocks — free fuel, zero
    emissions, no storage of their own. <strong>Geothermal, biomass, tidal</strong> fill
    regional niches. Each source answers to three numbers: its energy density (how much mass
    to carry), its capacity factor (the fraction of its rated output it actually delivers —
    nuclear ~90%, wind ~35%, solar ~15–25%, because night and clouds exist), and its emissions
    per unit energy (lifecycle: coal ~820 gCO₂/kWh; gas ~490; solar ~40; wind ~12; nuclear
    ~12). The transition is, in essence, moving the grid down that last column while holding
    the other two steady.
  </p>

  <h2>How the fuels formed: buried sunlight</h2>
  <p>
    The fossil menu is a geology lesson in disguise. <strong>Coal</strong> began as peat from
    the swampy forests of the Carboniferous (about 300 million years ago — the era that
    named the fuel), buried faster than it could decay; heat and pressure over deep time
    drove off water and volatiles, upgrading peat → lignite → bituminous coal → anthracite,
    increasing carbon content and energy density at each step. <strong>Oil and gas</strong>
    came from marine plankton settling into anoxic seafloor mud; burial cooked the organic-rich shale into liquid and gas hydrocarbons ("source rocks"), which migrated upward
    until trapped under impermeable "cap rock" in a fold, fault or salt dome — every oil
    field is a leak-proof geology container, which is why exploration is seismic
    prospecting. <strong>Uranium</strong> is mined from ore bodies (sandstone and
    unconformity deposits) where circulating fluids concentrated it. The timescale is the
    point: these are <em>stocks</em>, renewed on no human timescale, versus the
    <em>flows</em> (sun, wind, moving water) that cannot be depleted — the distinction that
    makes "renewable" a physical property, not a marketing one.
  </p>

  <h2>Extraction: where energy and mining meet</h2>
  <p>
    Every energy system begins with digging. <strong>Coal</strong> is strip-mined or
    deep-mined from sedimentary seams; mountaintop removal buries valley streams and its
    spoil leaches selenium and salinity for decades. <strong>Oil and gas</strong> come from
    drilling into trapped marine source rocks; <strong>fracking</strong> (hydraulic
    fracturing) unlocked "tight" shale reserves from 2000s — pumping water, sand and
    chemicals at pressures that crack the shale and hold the cracks open, letting gas flow.
    Fracking reshaped geopolitics (US oil output roughly doubled; the 2010s oil-price
    crash), and it carries a real but narrower risk record than its reputation: the
    documented problems are methane leakage (methane is ~30× CO₂'s warming power over a
    century — leaks can erase gas's climate advantage), induced earthquakes (Oklahoma —
    driven mostly by wastewater injection, the earthquakes lesson's case), and local water
    stress. The <strong>renewables-and-batteries</strong> pathway is also extractive: copper,
    lithium, cobalt, nickel and rare earths for generators and magnets. The honest
    comparison: a fossil plant burns its fuel forever (continuous mining), while a wind
    turbine is mined once (discrete materials) — but the mining still happens, still has
    locales (the Democratic Republic of Congo's cobalt, Chile's lithium brines), and still
    needs the governance — environmental standards, recycling, substitution research — that
    the resource unit's "sustainable management" heading exists to supply. The mitigation
    end of the extraction story is <strong>carbon capture and storage</strong>: capturing
    CO₂ at smokestacks (amine solvents bind it, heat releases it), compressing it, and
    injecting it into deep saline aquifers or depleted fields — operating at scale in
    Norway's North Sea since 1996 (Sleipner, about a million tonnes a year, monitored by
    seismic imaging). Capture is proven per tonne and expensive per plant; its honest role
    is the emissions with no other route (cement chemistry, the existing fleet) and
    negative emissions later in the century — not an excuse to keep burning, which its
    critics correctly police.
  </p>

  <h2>How the grid actually works</h2>
  <p>
    Electricity is generated as alternating current, <strong>transformed up</strong> to
    hundreds of kilovolts (higher voltage means lower current for the same power, and lower
    current means less resistive loss — transmission at 400 kV loses a few percent per
    hundred kilometres), shipped across the transmission network, transformed down through
    distribution, and consumed. The grid has almost no storage: <strong>supply must equal
    demand every second</strong>, and the balance is legible in one number — <em>frequency</em>
    (50 Hz in Europe, 60 Hz in the Americas). Draw more than you generate and frequency sags;
    generate surplus and it rises; drift too far and protection systems disconnect generators
    to save the hardware. Grid operators therefore run a continuous auction — forecasting
    demand (weather-driven, literally), scheduling plants, paying <em>balancing services</em>
    for reserve that can respond in seconds. The engineering vocabulary that follows:
    <strong>baseload</strong> (the always-on demand floor, historically served by big thermal
    plants), <strong>peaking</strong> (gas turbines for the evening surge), and
    <strong>intermittent</strong> renewables. The modern redesign replaces that stack with
    <strong>flexibility</strong>: storage, demand response (water heaters and EV chargers
    that shift their draw), interconnection over wide areas (wind is always blowing
    somewhere), and accurate forecasting — the grid lesson's core shift from "always-on
    supply" to "managed timing".
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "renewables can't power a modern grid because the sun
    sets." Individual windless nights are trivial; the real challenge is <em>correlated</em>
    lulls — weather systems that calm the wind over a whole region for a week — and
    seasonal balance (solar output halving in winter at high latitudes). Both are solvable
    with the toolkit above, at a cost that rises with ambition; the engineering question is
    never "possible?" but "at what price of firmness?" — and the answer keeps falling as
    storage, interconnection and forecasting improve.</p>

  <h2>The cost revolution, and what remains hard</h2>
  <p>
    The transition's economics flipped within one decade. Solar module prices fell about
    90% from 2010 to 2020 (a learning rate near 20% per doubling of capacity — the steepest
    cost curve of any energy technology ever measured); onshore wind fell by about
    two-thirds; batteries followed (about 90% down, 2010–2023). New solar and wind are now
    the <em>cheapest</em> electricity in most of the world — cheaper than running existing
    coal in a growing set of markets. What remains hard: <strong>storage duration</strong>
    (lithium batteries cover hours and dominate new installations; multi-day and seasonal
    storage needs pumped hydro, hydrogen, or demand flexibility),
    <strong>transmission</strong> (the best wind and sun are far from cities; new lines
    take a decade to approve — the grid, not generation, is now the bottleneck in several
    countries), <strong>firm clean power</strong> (nuclear, geothermal, hydro and gas with
    carbon capture cover the windless weeks; nuclear's cost overruns versus Korea's
    steady shipyard builds is the industry's lesson in learning curves), and
    <strong>electrification of the hard sectors</strong> — steel, cement, aviation, shipping
    — which need hydrogen, synthetic fuels or process redesign, not just clean electrons.
    Two worked numbers calibrate the enterprise. One gigawatt of constant power — a single
    large station — delivers about 8.8 billion kWh a year; supplying even a modest country
    with solar alone means hundreds of such gigawatts plus storage, which is why "the
    energy transition" is measured in decades of construction, not a decree. And the
    capacity-factor table explains the shape of real grids: one firm gigawatt-equivalent
    from 35%-capacity-factor wind means building about three gigawatts of turbines and the
    backup to match — the arithmetic behind every grid model's sprawl of rows.
  </p>

  <h2>Conservation and management: the cheapest watt</h2>
  <p>
    Efficiency is the resource nobody builds and everybody saves: the global energy
    intensity of GDP has fallen about 1–2% a year for decades, and every avoided kilowatt-hour
    avoids the generation, transmission and emissions attached to it. The levers, from
    cheapest up: <strong>building standards</strong> (heating and cooling are the largest
    building loads; insulation is permanent savings), <strong>appliance standards</strong>
    (LED lighting cut lighting's electricity demand roughly in half in a decade),
    <strong>industrial efficiency</strong> (waste-heat recovery, motor upgrades),
    <strong>demand management</strong> (time-of-use pricing shifting load to sunny hours —
    the grid's free storage), and <strong>electrification with efficiency</strong> (a heat
    pump delivers three to four units of heat per unit of electricity — a 300–400%
    "efficiency" that no furnace can match, because it moves heat rather than making it).
    Managed well, conservation buys the time that storage and transmission need to mature —
    which is why every credible net-zero pathway starts with the demand curve bending down
    before the supply stack turns green.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Reliability is a designed, continuously maintained property — and blackouts are its
    autopsies. Texas 2021 (Winter Storm Uri): a grid run mostly on gas failed in cold
    because wellheads and plant instrumentation were not weatherised — an infrastructure
    hardening failure, not a renewables failure, though wind turbines' icing took the
    political blame. The 2003 Northeast blackout: a sagging tree line in Ohio, a racing
    alarm bug, and operators without situational awareness cascaded 50 million people dark
    in minutes — the case that wrote modern reliability standards. Both reveal the deep
    truth the trilemma encodes: reliability is not a property of any generator but of the
    system's reserve margins, interconnections and operator drill. The transition's
    political economy is its hardest frontier: fossil jobs and tax bases concentrate in
    specific regions while clean-energy benefits diffuse nationally — the
    concentrated-pain pattern that has decided every energy transition's politics, and
    whose policy answer (transition funds, retraining, siting revenue) determines
    feasibility. And the numbers worth keeping: world electricity demand is heading toward
    roughly doubling by 2050 as transport and heat electrify — meaning the clean build-out
    must supply today's demand <em>plus</em> the switch, a fact that should size everyone's
    expectations of speed. Grid-scale batteries, HVDC interconnectors and heat-pump
    deployments are all compounding at 20–40% a year; the constraint is no longer
    invention but manufacturing, permitting and politics — the same trilemma, at a
    billion amperes.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> IRENA renewable cost data; IEA World Energy Outlook; NERC and ENTSO-E reliability reports; the thermodynamics and circuits lessons; Cambridge IGCSE Environmental Management 0680, unit 1.</p>
